A token-bucket rate limiter is a type of rate limiter that uses a fixed number of tokens to control the rate of access to a resource. The tokens are added to the bucket at a constant rate, and when a request comes in, the bucket is checked to see if there are enough tokens to allow the request. If there are enough tokens, the tokens are consumed and the request is allowed to proceed. If there are not enough tokens, the request is denied and the bucket is filled with new tokens until the request can be processed.
Here's an example of how a token-bucket rate limiter works in Go:
```go
package main
import (
	"fmt"
	"time"
)
type TokenBucket struct {
	tokens int
	rate   float64
}
func NewTokenBucket(tokens int, rate float64) *TokenBucket {
	return &TokenBucket{
		tokens: tokens,
		rate:   rate,
	}
}
func (t *TokenBucket) AddTokens(tokens int) {
	t.tokens += tokens
}
func (t *TokenBucket) ConsumeTokens(tokens int) {
	if t.tokens >= tokens {
		t.tokens -= tokens
	} else {
		fmt.Println("Insufficient tokens")
	}
}
